    "corpus_substrate_estimate": 32,
    "assessment": "The Coptic Kephalaia is a composite text with five distinguishable temporal layers. The oldest is an impersonal cosmological-correspondential substrate that systematically maps cosmic domains onto each other: zoomorphic forms onto realms, metals onto bodies, tastes onto worlds, sense-faculties onto storehouses, zodiac signs onto body parts, and cosmic figures onto emanation hierarchies. This substrate is heavily concentrated in Chapters 3-7 (systematic cosmic taxonomy), 10-19 (cosmic wars, seasons, releases), 27-37 (zodiac, vessels, cosmic structures), 44-56 (sea giant, conduits, fashioning of Adam, faculties), 69-72 (zodiac-body mapping, elements, garments), and 74-75 (living fire, summons/obedience). The strongest substrate signals are the zoomorphic-metal-taste correspondences (Ch. 6: lion-faced/copper, eagle-face/iron, fish-face/silver, dragon/lead), the five-element nomenclature (smoke-fire-wind-water-darkness), the soul-body binding formula (mind-in-bone, thought-in-sinew, insight-in-vein, counsel-in-flesh, consideration-in-skin in Ch. 38), and the body-cosmos structural mapping (head=Aries, torso=Cancer, belly=Virgo in Ch. 70). Four editorial layers overlay this substrate: (1) a DIALOGUE FRAME of formulaic question-and-answer sessions ('Once again the enlightener speaks... his disciples questioned him'); (2) EDITORIAL BRIDGES that connect cosmic categories to the contemporary world ('the spirit of the King of Fire is this one who reigns today in the sects that worship fire' — one side reaches outside the cosmic system); (3) INSTITUTIONAL material about church organization, catechumenate, fasting rules, alms practices, and apostolic succession narrative (especially Chs. 76-93, 99, 102-103, 105, 109, 115, 120-122); and (4) EXHORTATION/devotional closures ('Blessed is every one who will know them') that consistently appear at the END of substrate teaching sequences. The clearest seams occur at three boundary types: (a) where impersonal cosmic mapping transitions mid-paragraph to 'this is what reigns today in the sects' (editorial bridge, esp. Ch. 6 §132-136); (b) where systematic cosmological exposition ends with 'Blessed is he who will know these things' (exhortation closure); (c) where dialogue frame formulas wrap a substrate teaching unit. The substrate percentage is estimated at 32% — much of the text is substrate mixed with editorial extensions rather than pure substrate. Chapters 80-93 and 99-122 are predominantly institutional with minimal substrate content.",
    "total_bridge_phrases": 32,
    "total_institutional_terms": 22
  },
